                                        "entry_id": "a943b459-799a-3bf4-b3fa-588b3834a1b9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A building in Ancient Greece, housing the boule (council of citizens), where public affairs were discussed."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2ddfbc80-a70d-4de3-b331-6aba5713d98b",
                                                "sense_id": "db537d5f-f601-3922-bfc7-466de45bc627",
                                                "text": "[…]we print here an updated and revised list of bouleuteria, some attested only in written sources, some physically preserved.⁶³ In most cases, however, the identification of a building as a bouleuterion is uncertain."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9d4cd545-e80a-4438-9e4a-ca1e1d5aa604",
                                                "sense_id": "db537d5f-f601-3922-bfc7-466de45bc627",
                                                "text": "The \"Old Bouleuterion\", whether it was built before or after the Persian sack of Athens,³ certainly antedates the New Bouleuterion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "788acd79-4d81-41a9-9b3d-c0f4440bf29d",
                                                "sense_id": "db537d5f-f601-3922-bfc7-466de45bc627",
                                                "text": "The history of the bouleuterion as a distinct architectural form seems to begin in late Archaic Athens, where the first Bouleuterion was perhaps built soon after the constitutional reforms of Kleisthenes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
